Prediction:
- The lender must carry out an affordability assessment before deciding whether the mortgage is affordable.

- The assessment must consider whether you can afford the mortgage repayments:
  - At the outset / now; and
  - After the mortgage interest rate is expected to rise, not just at the initial rate.

- The lender must assess and include your existing financial commitments, including:
  - Your existing personal loan;
  - Any other secured borrowing already in place;
  - Any unsecured borrowing already in place;
  - Basic essential living expenses;
  - Other current commitments that affect your ability to make mortgage payments.

- The lender must take reasonable steps to verify your income and expenditure:
  - It should not rely only on what you state in the application;
  - It must consider evidence of your financial position.

- Because your income is variable, the lender must take a realistic approach:
  - It must assess whether your variable income is sustainable;
  - It must not assume unusually high future income unless there is supportable evidence for doing so.

- If the lender uses an interest-rate stress test or hypothetical rate rise:
  - The assumption must be applied consistently with responsible lending rules;
  - The lender must not use an unrealistic stress assumption;
  - It cannot simply choose unjustifiably high figures.

- If your application is refused on affordability grounds, the lender should be able to:
  - Explain the principal affordability factor that led to the refusal;
  - Retain enough evidence to show that the affordability assessment was properly carried out.
